  "query_preview": "../Sunday through Wednesday/...The front will continue\nto push eastward through Sunday, bringing rain to the southern\npanhandle beginning early in the morning and lasting through the\nnight. At the time of the Thursday night forecast and model\nguidance, the main concern Sunday is looking to be the winds for \nthe far southern panhandle. After the initial front, a meso low\nlooks to move in from the south into the eastern Gulf, sliding up\nalong the coastline from Haida Gwaii up into the panhandle. The\nuncertainty of this is largely how strong this feature will\ndevelop, and what track it will be as it moves up along the coast\nand into the panhandle. Some model guidance is hinting towards\nelevated winds and periods of higher gusts along the southern\ncoast of PoW, including Hydaburg, and for Annette Island, as well\nas elevated winds into Clarence Strait itself as the low passes\nclosely along the west coast of PoW. However, the lack of\nagreement on the path and strength of this meso low gives\nuncertainty on how high these winds will get, as well as if\ninstead the elevated winds will be further northward and impact\nmore along the central panhandle. It is currently expected that\nthe southern coastline may see a period of gusts up to 40 mph \nSunday, or higher if the solution with higher winds pans out.\nThere is higher confidence in the southern panhandle, including\nPoW and Ketchikan, seeing moderate rain rates with some periods \nof heavy rain on Sunday, with between 1 and 2 inches expected in \n24 hours.\n\nThis wetter and more active pattern is expected to continue into\nearly next week as, with the next system beginning to develop and\nmove into the Gulf and impact the panhandle Tuesday into \nWednesday.",
